.\" conn(1) is written by hand and built into conn: ? shows it in
.\" the workspace, and man conn reads the copy the install put beside
.\" the binary. The release stamps the .TH line with its version.
.TH CONN 1 "2026-09-21" "conn 0.16.1" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
conn \- a console for the processes working your projects
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B conn
.RI [ \-\-light | \-\-dark ]
.RI [ \-\-theme
.IR name ]
.br
.B conn down
.br
.B conn theme
.RB { claude | vim }
.br
.B conn \-\-help
.br
.B conn \-\-version
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B conn
shows every process working a project on this machine, filed by what it
needs from you, and puts the one you choose in front of you.
A process is a shell, an editor, a coding agent, a dev server, a
container, a Homebrew service, or anything else with a terminal whose
working directory is inside one of your projects.
.PP
On a machine with
.BR tmux (1),
the first
.B conn
brings up a tmux server of its own, on its own socket, with one window.
.B conn
is the
.I panel
on the left of that window, 44 columns wide.
The rest of the window is the
.IR workspace .
Work runs in windows of the server out of sight;
.B enter
on a row puts that process in the workspace and the keys in it.
A later
.B conn
attaches to the server that is up.
.B q
detaches;
the server and every process keep running.
.B conn down
takes the server down with everything in it.
.PP
One key,
.B ctrl\-space
by default, brings the keys back to the panel from inside any process.
It is the only key
.B conn
takes from tmux.
tmux's prefix is unset in
.BR conn 's
server; its other bindings do not apply.
.PP
Without tmux,
.B conn
shows the console and the processes view and can open or reach nothing.
Run with its output piped,
.B conn
prints the console once and exits.
.PP
.BR "conn readout" ,
.B conn hold
and
.B conn manual
are run by
.B conn
in panes of its server.
They are not for typing.
.
.SH THE VIEWS
.SS The console
The first screen.
The build and the station at the top, then what the machine and this
session are, then the start-up checks one by one, then the verdict.
Any key skips to the verdict; a key at the verdict continues to the
processes view.
.B c
brings the console back over the whole window.
See
.B THE CONSOLE
below.
.SS The processes view
The panel's own view.
Every process is a row, under the block of the project it works in.
A block is drawn with the project's name, a rule, and at the end of the
rule what the project wants of you, where it wants anything.
The rows are folded: what is only what its parent is doing is left off.
The cursor is on one row.
.B z
shows the whole process tree instead, unfolded, with a column for the
terminal and one for the age of the row's state; the band reads
.BR TREE .
The table is read again every two seconds.
.SS The page
While the keys are on the panel, the workspace holds the page: what
.B conn
knows of the row under the cursor.
It follows the cursor.
Nothing is pressed for it.
Any key in the page returns the keys to the panel.
See
.B THE PAGE
below.
.SS The projects list
.B p
opens the list of every project under the roots, with the processes
.B conn
holds a pane for under each, and a line to type into that narrows the
rows.
.SS The sessions view
.B A
opens the suspended Claude Code sessions at the cursor's project, with a
line to type into that narrows them.
.SS The settings
\fB,\fR opens
.BR conn 's
own configuration in the workspace, with the keys in it; the band reads
.BR SETTINGS .
The panel beside it stays the panel, reading the machine, and holds no
row under the cursor while they stand.
.B esc
closes them and puts the keys back where they were.
.PP
The rows are the roots the file names, each with what it turned out to
be on this machine, and a row that adds another.
.B enter
on a root types it over,
.B x
takes it out, and each is written to the file as it is made \(em only
the key it changes, the rest of the file carried through.
.B conn
walks what was written at once, on the next reading, rather than on the
next start: the panel reads the file every time it reads the process
table, and re-roots itself where it has changed.
Where
.B CONN_ROOTS
stands in front of the file the view says so, and the rows stay the
file's own.
A file that will not parse is shown and not written over.
.PP
Under the roots are the themes
.B conn
has, the one being worn marked
.BR "IN USE" ,
and the one the file names
.B "IN THE FILE"
where a
.B \-\-theme
on the way in put
.B conn
in another.
.B enter
wears one: the file is written, the server's mode with it, and the
server is dressed where it stands \(em the sixteen of every pane, the
panel's own ground, and the theme files for Claude Code and nvim where
those are already there.
.PP
Under the themes are the two grounds and
.BR "ASK THE TERMINAL" ,
which is what
.B conn
does with no ground in the file.
.B enter
on a ground wears it, the same road a theme takes, and writes
.B ground
to the file, so the next start comes up on it whatever the terminal
says.
.B enter
on
.B ASK THE TERMINAL
takes the key out again and changes nothing where the server stands:
OSC 11 is a question for a server rising, and a
.B conn
in a pane of its own server would be asking tmux, which answers with
the ground
.B conn
itself set.
A file naming a theme
.B conn
does not have, or a ground that is neither, is said under the heading
it belongs to.
.SS The roots view
Where a root is typed, with the directories on this machine that answer
what has been typed so far as its rows.
It is shown on the panel, instead of the processes view, when
.B conn
has been told no roots: it asks for one directory, writes it, and goes
to work.
The settings put the same line up in their own pane, for a root being
added or typed over, which
.B esc
leaves as it was.
.SS The manual
\fB?\fR opens this page in the workspace and puts the keys in it; the band
reads
.BR HELP .
The panel beside it holds the keys themselves: every key the panel
answers to, by what it acts on, which is this section on the screen.
.
.SH KEYS
What a key does depends on where the keys are.
The key bar across the foot of the window shows the keys that work
where the cursor is and nothing else.
.SS In a process
.TP 20
.B ctrl\-space
To the panel.
The row of the process you left goes under the cursor, so the next key
is about it.
.B CONN_KEY
names another key; see
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
.TP
.B ctrl\-space tab
To the process that has waited longest for you.
.TP
.B ctrl\-space ctrl\-space
To the process you were in before this one.
Pressed again, back.
.TP
.B ctrl\-space ?
The manual.
.SS On the panel, in the processes view
.TP 20
.BR j ", " k ", " down ", " up
Move the cursor a row.
The rows are a ring: past the last is the first.
.TP
.BR gg ", " G
The first row, the last row.
.TP
.B enter
Go in.
On a row with a pane: put the pane in the workspace and the keys in it.
On a container: its output, and on the next
.B enter
the pane holding it.
On a Homebrew service that is up: its log; down: start it.
On a declared process that is down: bring it up and go in.
A row
.B conn
only reports, with no pane of its own, takes no
.BR enter ,
and the bar does not offer it.
.TP
.B esc
Back into the last process the workspace held, wherever the cursor is.
.TP
.B tab
To the process that has waited longest for you.
Again: the next one, longest first, round.
.TP
.B x
End the row's process.
.B conn
asks first; see
.BR "With a question armed" .
.TP
.B s
A shell at the row's project.
On a container: a shell inside it.
.TP
.B S
The server's own client, where the row is a server
.B conn
knows:
.B psql
on a postgres.
The bar names the client.
.TP
.B o
The row's port in the browser, at
.IR localhost: port .
Offered where a row is running and has a port, whether it listens on
it or publishes it; a row that says several is opened at the lowest.
The scheme is the port's own:
.B conn
offers it a TLS handshake, and sends the browser to
.I https
where one comes back and
.I http
where none does.
The browser is the system's own:
.BR open (1)
on macOS,
.BR xdg\-open (1)
on Linux.
.TP
.B a
A new contact at the row's project: Claude Code, told it runs inside
.BR conn .
.TP
.B A
The sessions view for the row's project.
.TP
.B u
Bring up the row's declared process or Homebrew service.
The keys stay on the panel.
.TP
.B U
Bring up everything the row's project declares and does not have
running.
.TP
.B z
The whole tree, or the panel's blocks again.
.TP
.B c
The console.
.TP
.B p
The projects list.
.TP
.B ,
The settings.
.TP
.B ?
The manual.
.TP
.BR q ", " ctrl\-c
Detach.
Outside the server: quit.
.TP
.B left click
Put the cursor on the row under the pointer.
The wheel and a drag are tmux's: the wheel scrolls a pane, a drag
copies.
.SS On a line typed into
The projects list, the sessions view and the roots view each have a
line to type into.
A character types; the rows narrow to what the line says, and the
cursor goes to the first of them.
The line is edited as
.BR readline (3)
edits one in emacs mode:
.BR ctrl\-a ", " ctrl\-e ", " ctrl\-b ", " ctrl\-f ", " alt\-b ", " alt\-f ", " ctrl\-w ", " alt\-d ", " ctrl\-k ", " ctrl\-u ", " ctrl\-d .
The panel's letters are characters here, and its verbs move to
.BR alt .
.TP 20
.BR up ", " down ", " ctrl\-p ", " ctrl\-n
Move the cursor among the rows.
The rows are a ring: past the last is the first.
.TP
.B enter
Projects: on a project, open a shell there and return to the processes
view; on a process, go in.
Sessions: resume the session in a new pane and return to the processes
view.
Roots: take what was typed if it names a directory, else the directory
under the cursor, and write it to the file \(em over the root the
settings sent, or beside the ones already there.
From the settings, back to them; on the first start, go to work.
.TP
.B esc
Roots, from the settings: back, with nothing written.
.TP
.B tab
Roots only: fill the line with the directory under the cursor and a
slash.
.TP
.B alt\-s
Projects, sessions: a shell at the row's project.
.TP
.B alt\-a
Projects, sessions: a new contact at the row's project.
.TP
.B alt\-A
Projects, sessions: the sessions view for the row's project.
.TP
.BR alt\-u ", " alt\-U
Projects, sessions: bring up the row's project's declared processes.
.TP
.B esc
Back to the processes view.
If the list was opened with the key after
.BR ctrl\-space ,
back into the pane
.B ctrl\-space
was pressed in.
.TP
.B ctrl\-c
Detach.
Outside the server: quit.
.B q
is a character here.
.SS With a question armed
.B x
does not end anything.
It arms a question on the key bar, the command about to be run: the
band reads
.BR CONFIRM .
.TP 20
.B y
Run it.
.TP
.B any other key
Do not.
.PP
The questions, by row:
.TP 20
a process
.BI "kill \-TERM " "pid \(pc program" ?
A bare shell is sent
.B KILL
instead: shells ignore
.BR TERM .
On a shell whose rows are folded away, the question is about what the
shell runs, and the shell stays at its prompt.
.TP
a contact
As a process.
The contact is signalled, not what it runs; its child processes keep
going.
.TP
a container
.BI "docker stop " "id \(pc service" ?
.TP
a Homebrew service
.BI "brew services stop " "formula \(pc name" ?
.TP
a declared process, up
.BI "tmux send\-keys \-t " "pane " "C\-c \(pc " name ?
Its pane is taken down once the end is recorded.
.TP
a declared process, ended
.BI "kill\-pane " "pane \(pc name" ?
.PP
A row that reads
.B DOWN
takes no
.BR x .
.SS In the settings
.TP 20
.BR j ", " k ", " gg ", " G
Move the cursor a row.
The rows are a ring: past the last is the first.
.TP
.B enter
On a root: type it over.
On the row that adds one: type another.
On a theme or a ground: wear it.
On
.BR "ASK THE TERMINAL" :
leave the ground to the terminal from the next start.
.TP
.B x
Take the root under the cursor out of the file.
.TP
.B esc
On a root being typed: back to the rows, nothing written.
On the rows: close the settings.
The keys go back where they were before
.BR , .
.TP
.B ctrl\-space
Close them, to the panel.
.SS In the manual
.TP 20
.BR j ", " k ", " down ", " up ", " ctrl\-n ", " ctrl\-p
A line.
.TP
.BR space ", " ctrl\-f ", " b ", " ctrl\-b
A page.
.TP
.BR g ", " G
The top, the end.
.TP
.BR esc ", " q
Leave.
The keys go back where they were before
.BR ? .
.TP
.B ctrl\-space
Leave, to the panel.
.SS On the console
.TP 20
.B any key
Skip to the verdict; at the verdict, continue.
.TP
.BR q ", " ctrl\-c
Detach, or quit.
.
.SH WHAT A ROW SAYS
.SS Kinds
A row's kind is the program's name, in the tree view a column of its
own:
.TP 10
.B SHELL
zsh, bash, fish, sh, dash, nu, tcsh, ksh.
.TP
.B EDITOR
vim, nvim, vi, hx, helix, emacs, nano, micro, kak.
.TP
.B CONTACT
claude, codex, gemini, copilot, amp, opencode, aider.
A coding agent: a process that can stop and wait on you.
See
.BR CONTACTS .
.TP
.B SERVICE
A docker container, a Homebrew service, or a compose service a project
declares.
.TP
.B RUN
Anything else.
.SS Groups
.SS Blocks
In the panel a row is in the block of the project it works in.
Every block is at the margin, in the order of its project's path, and
named by the project's own directory; where two on the panel share that
name, as much of the path above it as tells them apart.
A project that holds other projects is not a block of its own here: it
is a heading in the tree, on
.BR z .
A project has a block while something in it is running.
What its
.I .conn
declares and is not running stands under it,
.BR DOWN .
A project nothing is up in is not listed: its rows would be a reading of
the file and not of the machine.
A
.I .conn
that would not read is a fault, and its project stands on that note
alone.
.PP
At the end of a block's rule is what the project wants of you, the
first of these it holds:
.TP 18
.B WAITING
A row is waiting.
The stamp blinks, and counts the rows where more than one waits.
.TP
.BI n " FAULTS"
A row is a fault: its own word where one row is, the count where more
are.
Stamped, holding still.
.TP
.B DOWN
A declared process is not running, counted the same way.
.PP
A project with none of these says nothing, and its rule runs to the
edge.
.SS Marks on a row
.TP 18
.B WAITING
The dot is the accent, the command is bold, and the row is stamped at
its right with how long it has waited.
.TP
.B WORKING
A spinner turns in the margin, before the dot.
.TP
serving
Alive, not a contact, and listening on a TCP port: the dot is the
running color and the ports stand at the row's right.
.TP
not running
.BR DOWN ,
.BR ENDED ,
.BR "EXIT " n .
The command is struck through and the row says the word at its right.
.TP
a fault
Stamped with its word at the right, holding still.
.PP
A row that is up with nothing to report says its ports at its right,
or nothing where it has none.
.SS Status
The status is the last word of a row in the tree view, and in the panel
what the row says at its right and what its block says of it.
The first that applies:
.TP 12
.B STOPPED
The kernel says the process is stopped: suspended with
.B ctrl\-z
or by a signal.
A fault.
.TP
.B ENDED
The kernel says the process is a zombie, or a declared process or a
container ended with status 0.
.TP
.BI "EXIT " n
A declared process, container or Homebrew service ended with status
.IR n .
A fault.
.TP
.B WAITING
A contact says it is waiting on you.
Read from Claude Code's own record of the session, never guessed.
.TP
.B CLOSED
The row was listening on a port and has had none for two readings
running, with the process still there: a server nobody can reach.
A fault.
Contacts and rows
.B conn
only reports are not watched this way, and a port bound again takes
the word off.
.TP
.B WORKING
The process spent at least a twentieth of one core's time since the
last reading two seconds ago; or a contact says it is busy.
The first reading calls nothing working.
.TP
.B IDLE
A contact says it is idle, or a shell with nothing under it.
.TP
.B ACTIVE
Anything else that is running.
.TP
.B DOWN
Declared and not running.
.PP
A container's status is docker's:
.BR STARTING ,
.BR UNHEALTHY ,
.BR RESTARTING ,
.B STOPPED
for a paused container,
.BR ACTIVE ,
.BR ENDED ,
.BR "EXIT " n .
A Homebrew service's is brew's:
.BR ACTIVE ,
.BR ERROR ,
.BR "EXIT " n ,
.BR DOWN .
.PP
A fault is drawn as a chip and holds still.
.B WAITING
blinks, and in the panel says the age of the wait in place of the word.
.B ACTIVE
and
.B IDLE
are said in the tree view only: in the panel a row up with nothing to
report says nothing.
The tree view dates each row by when
.B conn
last saw its status change, in one unit:
.BR 3D ,
.BR 2H ,
.BR 7M ,
.BR 41S .
.SS The command column
What a working contact is doing, when Claude Code has said: the tool
and its object, as
.IR "read tui.go" ,
.IR "edit main.go" ,
a shell command as typed,
.IR "grep pattern" ,
.IR "agent description" .
Otherwise, for a contact, the title of its session.
Otherwise what a folded shell runs.
Otherwise the command as typed.
A contact's command line is shown without the note
.B conn
handed it or the session it resumed.
.SS Folding
The panel folds the tree.
A row survives if it is the head of its pane, a contact, a service, a
listener, a fault,
.BR WAITING ,
or
.BR DOWN .
Every other row folds into the nearest surviving ancestor.
A shell that folded rows away shows what it runs.
One listener under a head folds into the head, and the head carries its
ports.
.B z
shows every row.
.SS Ports
TCP listeners only, lowest first.
UDP and unix sockets are on the page.
.B o
opens the lowest in the browser.
A row whose ports all go while its process lives says
.BR CLOSED .
.PP
In the panel the ports end at the panel's right edge, so that the rows
that serve are read down that edge rather than found along each row.
The right of a row is one column: a row waiting, at fault or over says
its word there instead, since where a row would have taken you is not
the thing to know about a row that is asking for you or has stopped.
In the tree the ports follow the command, the tree having columns of
its own.
.SS Project names
In the tree a project is named with its root stripped:
.I ~/projects/w0zro/conn
under the root
.I ~/projects
is
.IR w0zro/conn .
Work in a directory outside every root is under
.B ~
and the path.
A directory that groups two or more projects is a heading over them in
the tree; the panel has no heading for it.
.SS Marks
The cursor's row is on raised ground.
The row whose pane is in the workspace carries a bar in the margin.
A row
.B conn
only reports, with no pane of its own, is dimmed.
.SS Notes under the rows
.TP 4
\(bu
.B NO PROCESSES
.TP
\(bu
.BI "THE PROCESS TABLE COULD NOT BE READ: " why
.TP
\(bu
.B DOCKER NOT ANSWERING \(pc AS LAST SEEN
.TP
\(bu
.BI ".CONN: LINE " "n" ": " why
.TP
\(bu
What the server would not do, in its words, until the next key:
.IB "the shell could not be opened: " why
and the like.
.TP
\(bu
.BI "... " n " ABOVE \(pc " n " BELOW"
when the rows do not fit.
.
.SH PROJECTS AND ROOTS
A root is a directory
.B conn
looks for projects under.
The roots are
.B CONN_ROOTS
if set, else
.B roots
in the configuration file.
There is no default.
Told no roots,
.B conn
asks for one on the roots view before showing any processes, and writes
the answer to the configuration file.
The settings view is where they are edited afterwards.
.PP
A project is a git repository under a root, or a directory under a root
that directly holds one.
A process belongs to the nearest project at or above its working
directory.
A process with no terminal is listed under a project only while
terminal work is happening in that project.
.PP
The projects list walks the roots when opened.
It does not enter a repository, a directory whose name begins with a
dot,
.IR node_modules ,
.IR vendor ,
.IR target ,
.IR .build ,
.IR Pods ,
.IR .venv ,
.IR venv ,
or a directory holding a
.IR CACHEDIR.TAG .
A directory holding two or more repositories is a group with a count of
.BR "N REPOS" .
The header counts
.B N FOUND
or
.BR "N OF M"
under a filter.
The filter matches a project by name and a process by kind and
activity.
The list also reads the process table while it is up, so a process row
in it is current.
.PP
Words the list can show:
.BR SCANNING ,
.BI "NOTHING ANSWERS TO " filter ,
.BI "NO REPOSITORIES UNDER " roots ,
.BR "CONN HAS NO ROOTS" ,
.BI "THE ROOTS COULD NOT BE WALKED: " why ,
.BI "THE CONFIG COULD NOT BE READ: " why .
.
.SH DECLARING PROCESSES
A project may keep a file named
.I .conn
at its top, listing the processes that work it.
One process a line:
.PP
.nf
.RS
.IB name " [" dir "]: " command
.RE
.fi
.PP
.I name
is one word of letters, digits, dot, underscore and hyphen.
.I dir
is a directory under the project, where the process runs; the project
itself if left out.
.I command
is a shell line, handed to
.B sh
as written.
Blank lines and lines beginning with
.B #
are ignored.
.PP
.nf
.RS
# what works this project
web:            npm run dev
api  services:  go run ./cmd/api
db:             docker compose up postgres
cache:          brew services start redis
.RE
.fi
.PP
A declared process that is not running is a row of the project's block,
worded
.BR DOWN ,
at the foot of the rows of what is running.
A project with nothing running in it shows nothing of its file, and is
not a block at all.
.PP
.BR enter ,
.B u
or
.B U
brings a declaration up in a pane of the server, marked as the
declaration; the row carries the declared name.
When it ends, the pane holds its output, and the row reads
.B ENDED
or
.BI "EXIT " n .
A process started by hand with the declared command in the declared
directory is the declaration, and is not brought up a second time.
.PP
A command that runs
.B docker compose up
gives one
.B SERVICE
row per service compose would bring up, down until it runs.
A command that runs
.B brew services start
or
.B run
gives one
.B SERVICE
row for the formula; see
.BR SERVICES .
.PP
Errors in the file are a note under the project's rows, naming the
line:
.BR "want name [dir]: command" ,
.IB word " is not a name" ,
.IB dir " is not under the project" ,
.IB dir " is not a directory" ,
.IB name " runs nothing" ,
.IB name " is declared twice" .
.
.SH CONTACTS
A contact is a coding agent: a process that works on its own and stops
to ask you something.
.B conn
recognises the programs listed under
.BR Kinds ,
but reads the state of one only:
Claude Code.
Every other contact reads as any process does.
.PP
.B a
starts Claude Code at the cursor's project.
.B conn
appends a note to its system prompt saying that it runs inside
.B conn
and how to start long-lived processes in a window of the server rather
than in the background.
The note is not shown on the row.
.PP
Claude Code records each running instance in a file under its
configuration directory.
.B conn
reads it, trusts it only if it was written by the process on the row,
and takes its word:
.B busy
and
.B shell
are
.BR WORKING ,
.B waiting
is
.BR WAITING ,
.B idle
is
.BR IDLE .
For a
.B WAITING
row the page reads the question itself from the session transcript.
.PP
A contact's row is labelled with its session's title, which Claude
Code writes into the transcript from the first prompt and replaces on
.BR /rename .
While the contact is working, the row says what it is doing instead.
.PP
.B tab
goes to the contact that has waited longest.
.B x
on a contact signals the contact and not what it runs.
.PP
A suspended session is a Claude Code transcript at a project that no
running contact is carrying.
.B A
lists them, newest first, each with its branch, its last prompt and its
age.
The line typed into matches the prompt, the branch and the directory.
.B enter
resumes the session with
.B claude \-\-resume
in a new pane, at the session's own directory.
With none:
.BR "NOTHING SUSPENDED HERE" .
.
.SH SERVICES
.SS Docker
A container started by compose is a
.B SERVICE
row under the project of its compose working directory, under the
.B docker compose up
that runs it where there is one.
A container without a compose working directory is not listed.
A container that has stopped is listed while a sibling runs or a
compose runs in its directory.
The row carries the ports the container publishes.
.B conn
watches
.B docker events
and lists containers as they change; it does not poll.
When docker does not answer, the rows are as last seen and the view
says so.
.PP
.B enter
opens the container's output; the next
.B enter
goes back to the pane holding it.
.B s
opens a shell inside the container.
.B S
opens the server's client inside it, where the image is one
.B conn
knows.
.B x
asks to
.B docker stop
it.
.SS Homebrew
A project claims a Homebrew service by declaring it; see
.BR "DECLARING PROCESSES" .
While any project does,
.B conn
asks
.B brew services
every fifteen seconds, with analytics off.
The row carries the service's pid and ports from the process table.
A formula two projects declare is one row, with
.B *
for its project.
.PP
.B enter
on a service that is up tails its log; down, starts it.
.B u
starts it.
.B x
asks to
.B brew services stop
it.
.
.SH THE PAGE
The page is what
.B conn
knows of the row under the cursor, in the workspace, following the
cursor.
It is up while the keys are on the panel in the processes view, the
projects list or the sessions view, and stands aside for whatever else
is put in the workspace: a process gone into, the manual, the
settings.
.SS A process
Groups, each with the fields it has:
.TP 10
.B WAITING
On, For, Asks or Said.
.TP
.B WHAT
Kind, Command, Status and for how long, State
.RI ( Running ,
.IR Sleeping ,
.IR Stopped ,
whether it has the terminal), Up, CPU.
.TP
.B WHERE
Project, CWD, TTY, Pane and whether it can be reached.
.TP
.B SOCKETS
Listens, Connected, Unix.
.TP
.B CONTACT
With, Model, Context, Session, Name, Version, Running, Branch, Last ask.
.TP
.B PROJECT
Branch and how many files changed, Commit, Committed, Tracking and how
far ahead or behind.
.TP
.B TREE
Parent, Runs.
.SS A contact
A handoff sheet.
.B CONTACT
with the session's designation, the project and branch, the version
and the context carried.
.B WAITING FOR YOU
with the question, how long it has waited, and the tool it was asked
with.
.B PROCEDURE
with the keys that answer it.
.B CAUTION
with what
.B x
does and does not end.
.B HOW THE WORK HAS GONE
as a bar of time worked against time at rest, waiting or idle, and the
last prompt you gave it. The work counts to the moment it stopped, and
not a second past it: a contact doing nothing stands still on the page,
and only the rest beside it grows.
.B SPECIFICATIONS
with the rest.
.SS A service, a project, a session
A container's page adds Service, Image, Health, Since, Compose, Ports.
A Homebrew service's adds Formula and Log.
A project's page is its git state and what runs in it.
A session's page is its branch, its last prompt and the command that
resumes it.
.
.SH THE CONSOLE
.SS Header
The wordmark, the version, with
.B (devel)
for a build not exactly a release; the station as
.IR USER@HOST ;
the clock in UTC; the build's commit, date, and
.B MODIFIED
if the tree was dirty.
.SS MACHINE
SYSTEM, KERNEL, MODEL, CPU, MEMORY, SWAP, VOLUME, UPTIME, PROCESSES,
SIP.
.SS SESSION
USER, SHELL, TTY, TERMINAL, SESSION, LOCALE, TIME ZONE, CWD, PROCESS,
ENV, RUNTIME, BINARY.
.SS START-UP CHECKS
Each check is a line ending in its status.
.B NOMINAL
is quiet; anything else is a chip and blinks.
.TP 10
.B SCREEN
Columns by rows, the terminal, and whether it is truecolor.
.B SMALL
under 80 columns or the rows the console needs.
.B UNCHECKED
with no terminal.
.TP
.B STATE
The state directory.
.BR "NOT A DIR" ,
.BR "READ ONLY" ,
.BR "NO PATH" .
.TP
.B CONFIG
The configuration file, with
.B CONN_ROOTS IN FORCE
when the environment stands in front of it.
.B NO FILE
if absent,
.B NOT READ
if it will not parse,
.B NO ROOTS
if it names none,
.B NO THEME
if it names a theme
.B conn
does not have,
.B NO GROUND
if it names a ground that is neither dark nor light.
.TP
.B ROOT
One per root.
.B MISSING
if the directory is not there, which is not a fault;
.B NOT A DIR
if it is not a directory.
.TP
.B DISK
Free space on the volume.
.B LOW
under a tenth of the volume, floored at 5 GB and capped at 50 GB.
.TP
.B MEMORY
The kernel's pressure verdict on macOS:
.B WARNING
or
.BR CRITICAL .
Elsewhere
.B LOW
under ten percent available.
.TP
.B LOAD
The three load averages and the core count.
.B HIGH
when the one-minute load exceeds the cores.
.TP
.B NET
Interfaces up and the address.
.B DOWN
with none up, or none with a default route.
.TP
.B POWER
Battery or mains.
.B LOW
discharging under ten percent.
.TP
.B CLOCK
.B BEHIND
if the clock is before the build's date.
.TP
.BR TMUX ", " LSOF
Where each was found.
.B MISSING
otherwise.
There is no
.B LSOF
check on Linux.
.SS Verdict
.B ALL SYSTEMS NOMINAL
or
.BI "N SYSTEMS NOT NOMINAL" .
.SS Too small
Under 80 columns or too few rows, the console shows the screen size,
.BR SMALL ,
and the size it needs.
.
.SH THE STATUS LINE
Two rows across the foot of the window, drawn by tmux from what
.B conn
tells it.
.SS The band
At the left, the wordmark, or the state the keys are in:
.B TREE
in the tree view,
.B HELP
in the manual,
.B CONFIRM
with a question armed,
.B COPY
in tmux's copy mode.
At the right, how long
.B conn
has been up, as
.BR "T+ 02h 13m" .
.SS The key bar
The keys that work where the cursor is, each with a word.
At the right, the host and the version.
.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP 18
.B conn
The console, then the processes view.
In the server: attach.
.TP
.B conn down
Take the server down with every process in it.
Prints one line per window ended, and one for the server.
.TP
.B conn theme claude
Write
.BR conn 's
theme for Claude Code to Claude Code's themes directory as
.BR custom:conn ,
on the ground the server is on.
If Claude Code is on one of its own themes,
.B conn
offers to switch it.
If it is on a custom theme,
.B conn
says so and leaves it.
.TP
.B conn theme vim
Write
.BR conn 's
colorscheme for nvim to its colors directory as
.BR conn ,
on the ground the server is on.
nvim takes it with
.BR "colorscheme conn" .
.B CONN
is set in the server's environment, so a config can reach for it under
.B conn
and keep its own elsewhere.
.TP
.B conn \-\-help
The synopsis.
.TP
.B conn \-\-version
The version and the build.
.PP
Once written, both theme files are rewritten whenever a server comes up
on a different ground.
.
.SH OPTIONS
Given before a command.
.TP 14
.B \-\-light
The ground is light.
.TP
.B \-\-dark
The ground is dark.
.TP
.BI \-\-theme " name"
The theme:
.B conn
or
.BR datum .
.PP
For a server coming up, these decide the ground and the theme.
For a server already up, they change it: every pane of
.BR conn 's
own is redrawn.
Otherwise the ground is fixed for the server's life.
Without a flag, a fresh server takes
.B ground
from the configuration file, and with none there asks the terminal its
background with OSC 11, calling it dark when its luminance is under a
half or when the terminal does not answer within 200 ms.
.B \-\-light
with
.B \-\-dark
is refused, as are two
.BR \-\-theme s.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CONN_ROOTS
The roots, separated as
.B PATH
is.
Stands in front of the configuration file.
.TP
.B CONN_KEY
The panel key, in tmux's spelling:
.B C\-Space
by default.
.TP
.B CONN_SOCKET
The server's socket.
Default: under the state directory.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the configuration directory is.
Default
.IR ~/.config .
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Where the state directory is.
Default
.IR ~/.local/state .
.TP
.B CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR
Where Claude Code keeps its files.
Default
.IR ~/.claude .
.TP
.B COLORTERM
.B truecolor
or
.B 24bit
makes
.B conn
draw in truecolor.
.PP
In the server,
.B conn
sets
.BR CONN=1 ,
.B COLORTERM=truecolor
and
.BR CLAUDE_CODE_TMUX_TRUECOLOR=1 .
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/conn/config.json
The configuration.
Three keys, each of which may be left out:
.PP
.nf
.RS
{
  "roots": ["~/projects", "~/work"],
  "theme": "conn",
  "ground": "dark"
}
.RE
.fi
.IP
A leading
.B ~
in a root is your home.
.B conn
reads this file and writes
.BR roots ,
.B theme
and
.BR ground ,
from the settings view, keeping every other key.
No
.B ground
is the terminal's own, asked when a server rises.
A file that will not parse is a fault on the console and is never
written over.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/conn/
The state directory:
.I tmux.sock
the server's socket;
.I tmux.conf
what
.B conn
tells tmux;
.I mode
the ground and theme the server came up in;
.I tmux.sock.cursor
the row under the cursor, for the page;
.I conn.1
this page, written for
.BR man .
.TP
.I ~/.claude/sessions/
Claude Code's record of each running instance, read for a contact's
status.
.TP
.I ~/.claude/projects/
Claude Code's transcripts, read for a waiting contact's question and for
suspended sessions.
.TP
.I ~/.claude/themes/conn.json
Written by
.BR "conn theme claude" .
.TP
.I ~/.claude/settings.json
Read by
.B conn theme claude
for the theme in use; its
.B theme
key rewritten only with your consent.
.TP
.I ~/.config/nvim/colors/conn.vim
Written by
.BR "conn theme vim" .
.TP
.I .conn
At a project's top: its declared processes.
.
.SH THEMES
.B conn
has two themes,
.B conn
and
.BR datum ,
each with a dark and a light ground.
The theme is
.B theme
in the configuration file,
.BR \-\-theme ,
or the settings view, where picking one dresses the server where it
stands.
The ground is
.B ground
in the configuration file,
.BR \-\-light / \-\-dark ,
or the settings view; with none of those it is asked of the terminal
when a server rises.
Every color is drawn by its role, and the sixteen ANSI colors of every
pane in the server are set to the theme's.
.
.SH TMUX
.B conn
runs its own tmux server on its own socket.
A tmux of yours on the default socket is not touched.
In
.BR conn 's
server:
.TP 4
\(bu
The prefix is none.
One key is bound, the panel key, in the root table.
.TP
\(bu
The mouse is on: the wheel scrolls a pane, a drag copies, a click on a
row of the panel moves the cursor.
The border cannot be dragged; the panel keeps its width.
.TP
\(bu
Copy mode uses vi keys.
The band reads
.B COPY
while a pane is in it.
.TP
\(bu
Each pane keeps 10000 lines of history.
.TP
\(bu
The status line is
.BR conn 's,
two rows at the foot.
Windows have no tabs.
.PP
A tmux command typed by hand reaches the server with
.BI "tmux \-S " socket ;
the socket is under
.B FILES
and in the note
.B conn
hands each contact.
.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.TP
.BI "conn: no such command: " name
Followed by the synopsis.
Exit 2.
.TP
.B conn: \-\-dark and \-\-light are a contradiction
Exit 2.
.TP
.BI "conn: \-\-theme " a " and \-\-theme " b " are a contradiction"
Exit 2.
.TP
.BI "conn: conn has no theme " name "; it has conn and datum"
Exit 2.
.TP
.BI "conn: the tmux server could not be brought up: " why
.B conn
goes on without a server.
.TP
.B conn: tmux is not on PATH; there is no server to take down
Exit 1.
.TP
.BI "conn: no server up on " socket
From
.BR "conn down" .
Exit 0.
.TP
.BI "conn theme: say which program: conn theme claude, conn theme vim"
Exit 1.
.TP
.BI "conn theme: conn has no theme for " name
Followed by the two it has.
Exit 1.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 when a command failed; 2 on a usage error.
.
.SH CAVEATS
.TP 4
\(bu
.B x
on a bare shell sends
.BR KILL .
.TP
\(bu
.B x
on a contact signals the contact, not the processes under it.
.TP
\(bu
The ports column shows TCP listeners only.
.TP
\(bu
A process with no terminal is listed only under a project where
terminal work is happening.
.TP
\(bu
A container appears only if compose labelled it with a working
directory.
.TP
\(bu
Homebrew is asked only while some project declares a service, and every
fifteen seconds.
.TP
\(bu
Only Claude Code's state is read.
Other contacts read as any process.
.TP
\(bu
The ground is fixed for the server's life.
.BR \-\-light ,
.B \-\-dark
and the settings view change it on a running server;
.B conn down
asks the terminal again, unless the file names a ground.
.TP
\(bu
.B q
detaches.
The server and every process keep running until
.BR "conn down" .
.TP
\(bu
On Linux there is no
.B LSOF
check, and clock ticks are assumed to be 100 a second.
On macOS a process of another user has no arguments.
.TP
\(bu
The tree is read every two seconds.
A process that lives shorter is never seen.
.
